Z||000000||AL||11-06-1999||02:04 PM||||<BR>check this out, i changed the sensor <BR>next to the intake and i also grounded <BR>out the wires and they work but i can't <BR>seem to get a reading on my gage to tell <BR>me what tmp i'm at.i've also tried <BR>another sensor(both from gm)and nothing, <BR>any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Wolfhound||11-06-1999||09:36 PM||||What year and engine?||||reg||1
Z||000002||88formula||11-06-1999||11:46 PM||||Temp sensors can only read temperature from a liquid, if there is air trapped were the sensor is, it wont work. I'v experienced this with some Cadillacs.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fierospeeder||11-07-1999||04:27 AM||||if you got a cheap mulitimeter just hook it up and check it when the engine is cold and see what the resistance is, then run your engine and see if the resistance is changing.  If it does you know the sensor is fine and something is wrong with the wiring going to your gauge.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bill Osmer||11-07-1999||06:52 PM||||Also, if your connector shell has self-destructed (like mine), then you will have two separate connector pins to plug back onto the temp sender. If they're on the wrong pins, then your temp gauge won't work but your IP light will begin glowing a little as your temp comes up to normal. Just change the two pins around, and all will be well agian (I learned this because I hooked them up wrong the 1st time!)||||reg||1
